WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:03.860
JONATHAN SHNEIER: Hi, everyone.

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:04.360
Welcome.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:06.380
Thanks for coming.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.340
Thanks for those of you who read
all the way through the title

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:11.860
and still came.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:12.700
My name is Jonathan.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.992
I'm a software engineer
on the Google Doodle team,

00:00:14.992 --> 00:00:16.450
and we're here to
talk about how we

00:00:16.450 --> 00:00:20.050
built our first big multiplayer
game, the 2018 Halloween

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.160
Doodle.

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:25.810
And there we go.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:26.480
OK.

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:28.740
So we've divided our
story into three parts.

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.220
I'm going to give you some
background and talk about how

00:00:31.220 --> 00:00:33.320
we approach the
design process, then

00:00:33.320 --> 00:00:35.660
Joseph is going to talk about
designing the Open Match

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:37.010
matchmaker.

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:39.110
And then Brian is
going to talk about how

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:43.030
we scaled up and launched
our production backends.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:47.560
So Doodles, who are we and
what are we talking about?

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.760
Our job is basically to use
technology to make Google fun,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.790
to surprise and
delight our users.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.340
When we talk about
Doodles, we mean this--

00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:00.890
the illustrations,
animations, and games that

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:03.630
replace the Google logo to
celebrate notable people,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.600
events, and holidays.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:07.520
Here are a few examples
of ways we've explored

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:09.410
web technology in the past.

00:01:09.410 --> 00:01:11.255
Our first game was
"PAC-MAN" in 2010.

00:01:11.255 --> 00:01:13.040
It's still one of
our most popular.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.980
Nothing like peaking early.

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.870
In 2012, we built
the synthesizer

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:22.190
to celebrate Robert Moog using
the then new Web Audio API.

00:01:22.190 --> 00:01:23.780
People can make four
track recordings

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:26.140
and share them
with their friends.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:29.010
In 2016, we decided to
try mobile development.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:31.330
To celebrate the Olympics,
we built a series

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:36.450
of sports mini games into the
Google App on Android and iOS.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:38.580
So that brings us to
Halloween 2018, which

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:40.590
is the subject of this talk.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:42.390
For this Doodle we
decided to tackle

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.125
a new challenge, multiplayer.

00:01:44.125 --> 00:01:46.300
The whole world searches
on Google every day.

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.300
What if we could give all
these people a fun way

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.030
to interact with each other?

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:53.760
So right off the bat, we had
a few problems to deal with.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:56.700
First off, we're not
multiplayer game developers.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:00.780
All of our previous games ran
entirely in the user's browser.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.270
We don't know how to build and
run a fleet of game servers,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.480
and we don't have any
experience with cloud platforms.

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:09.180
That's unfortunate because
we have a lot of users,

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.970
and we're going to
need a lot of servers.

00:02:11.970 --> 00:02:13.860
Also, most of our
users aren't gamers

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:15.540
or, at least, they
didn't come to play.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:17.500
They came to search.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.800
And finally, we're
a very small team.

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:24.360
How are we possibly going to
pull off such a big project?

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.920
Well, we did anyway.

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.230
We ran for two days
in 62 countries,

00:02:28.230 --> 00:02:29.850
and we got 100 million players.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.000
So how did we get there?

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.730
First thing we had to learn was
how to do multiplayer at all.

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.660
So we had a hackathon to see
what we could do in a week.

00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:45.005
We ended up with this tank
combat capture the flag game.

00:02:45.005 --> 00:02:46.820
We had a play test at
the end of the week,

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:49.700
and we knew we had something
when people started shouting

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:52.320
at each other across the room.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.590
So now we had some idea
of how to do multiplayer,

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:56.960
and we needed to focus on
the second problem, which is

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:59.390
how to design for our audience.

00:02:59.390 --> 00:03:01.460
Our target demographic
is everyone

00:03:01.460 --> 00:03:05.050
who uses the internet,
which is pretty broad.

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.590
And we generally go
for cute art styles,

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.418
and try to find simple mechanics
that are fun for everyone.

00:03:10.418 --> 00:03:12.210
And the way we do that
is through a process

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:15.355
of rapid prototyping
and frequent play tests.

00:03:15.355 --> 00:03:16.730
Here's a couple
of the prototypes

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:17.772
that didn't make the cut.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:22.330
So what does that mean for
our multiplayer Doodle?

00:03:22.330 --> 00:03:23.890
Because our users
didn't necessarily

00:03:23.890 --> 00:03:26.830
come seeking a game, we
wanted to get them in and out

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:28.610
as quickly as possible.

00:03:28.610 --> 00:03:31.460
We also wanted to avoid direct
user-to-user interactions

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:32.930
for a couple of reasons.

00:03:32.930 --> 00:03:35.657
First, PvP is not
really on-brand for us.

00:03:35.657 --> 00:03:37.240
Remember, this whole
game is basically

00:03:37.240 --> 00:03:39.670
a glorified corporate logo.

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:41.793
So second, we wanted
a mechanic that

00:03:41.793 --> 00:03:43.210
wouldn't be
frustrating for people

00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:47.050
on slow, unreliable, or
high latency networks.

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:49.960
Finally, we wanted to keep the
backends as simple as possible,

00:03:49.960 --> 00:03:51.510
both because of
the massive scale

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.670
and because this was our
first time trying this.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:57.067
The project was
ambitious enough already.

00:03:57.067 --> 00:03:58.650
So these considerations
led us to make

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:02.340
some technical decisions, which
shaped the design of the game.

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:04.830
We'd have short,
but exciting matches

00:04:04.830 --> 00:04:08.250
with relatively few players,
but massive numbers of them

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:11.790
using highly parallelizable
game server instances.

00:04:11.790 --> 00:04:14.982
We'd keep matchmaking
complexity to a minimum.

00:04:14.982 --> 00:04:17.190
We just wanted players to
play on the closest servers

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.740
to keep latency down, and we
could rely on the Cloud Load

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:21.930
Balancer to handle that for us.

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:24.960
We didn't need fancy skill-based
matchmaking or stat-keeping,

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:27.900
since it's a super casual game,
and it's only live for two days

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.700
anyway.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.040
Game server instances
could be short-lived.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.160
Since there's no player
progression or stateful world,

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:37.020
we could create
and destroy servers

00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:40.480
as needed to meet player demand.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:42.670
Our first prototype
architecture was as simple

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:44.470
as we could possibly make it.

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:46.510
We used Node.js
for our game server

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:48.610
so that we could write
everything in JavaScript,

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.220
and share code between
client and server.

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:53.770
Communications were
handled by Websockets,

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.860
and we kept the client
as simple as possible.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:57.430
It sent player
input to the server

00:04:57.430 --> 00:04:59.650
and rendered server
updates as they arrived.

00:04:59.650 --> 00:05:03.590
All the game logic
happened on the server.

00:05:03.590 --> 00:05:05.420
At this point, we had
a working prototype,

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:06.950
but it started to
become apparent

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:11.300
that our system didn't scale
well past about one developer.

00:05:11.300 --> 00:05:14.570
We were running game servers
by SSHing into a Cloud VM

00:05:14.570 --> 00:05:17.170
and running Node on
the command line.

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:19.870
Each developer manually
managed their own single VM,

00:05:19.870 --> 00:05:23.650
and we had no automation, or
configuration, or maintenance,

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:27.310
and no easy way to scale
up to more servers.

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:29.590
So we needed to figure
out how to do it for real.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.500
We started researching
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:34.630
Mark Mandel, who you'll
hear from if you come back

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:37.510
for the next talk,
introduced us to Kubernetes

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:40.300
via his blog series, "Scaling
Dedicated Game Servers

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.758
with Kubernetes."

00:05:41.758 --> 00:05:44.290
Kubernetes would solve
several problems.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:46.540
It would give us an easy way
to configure, and manage,

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.060
and scale our game
servers, and it would also

00:05:49.060 --> 00:05:50.920
help with the
development process.

00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.730
Once we had containerized
the game servers,

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:54.813
we could easily run in the
same containers locally

00:05:54.813 --> 00:05:57.370
and in the cloud, which brought
our development environment

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:00.070
much closer to production.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.170
This allowed us to separate
backend infrastructure

00:06:02.170 --> 00:06:06.137
development from development
of the game itself.

00:06:06.137 --> 00:06:07.720
So here's what things
looked like once

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.400
we had converted to Kubernetes.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:11.920
Game clients connect
to a matchmaker.

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:13.240
This is not yet Open Match.

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.160
It's just a very
basic Node server

00:06:15.160 --> 00:06:17.312
that keeps a queue
of clients in memory.

00:06:17.312 --> 00:06:19.270
When enough clients are
queued to form a match,

00:06:19.270 --> 00:06:21.340
the matchmaker uses
the Kubernetes API

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:23.190
to launch a new game server.

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.480
The clients then connect
directly to the game server

00:06:25.480 --> 00:06:26.680
and they play.

00:06:26.680 --> 00:06:30.990
When the match is over, the
game server shuts itself down.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:33.490
So this architecture was good
enough to let us scale up

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.500
and begin load testing.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:38.200
This allowed us to begin to
estimate the resources we'd

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:39.940
need for a full
scale production,

00:06:39.940 --> 00:06:42.050
and to answer a very
important question--

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:46.040
can we actually afford
to do this launch at all?

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.210
It also let us begin doing some
debugging of client and server

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:51.400
behavior at scale, making
sure the system behaved

00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:53.890
well in the face of client
disconnects and reconnects,

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:55.480
clients who went
through matchmaking,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.580
but never connected to
the game servers, clients

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.680
on bad networks-- all
the fun stuff that

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:02.228
happens in the real world.

00:07:02.228 --> 00:07:03.770
So things were
starting to look good,

00:07:03.770 --> 00:07:05.330
but we still had a
lot of questions.

00:07:05.330 --> 00:07:08.120
And we knew our homespun
matchmaker couldn't scale,

00:07:08.120 --> 00:07:10.382
so we called in
the professionals.

00:07:13.826 --> 00:07:15.302
JOSEPH HOLLEY: Thank you, sir.

00:07:20.320 --> 00:07:21.330
Hello.

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:25.800
I'm Joseph, and I work
for Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.740
The other two gentlemen
you'll hear from today

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:31.860
both worked on the Doodle.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:36.420
So Google is working
with the games industry.

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:39.570
We're focused on helping
developers succeed,

00:07:39.570 --> 00:07:43.050
and Cloud is really at the
center of those efforts.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:45.390
We want to help you
create great games,

00:07:45.390 --> 00:07:48.640
connect with players globally,
and scale your business.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:50.640
So we're working with all
the other Google teams

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.370
to try and bring
that vision to life.

00:07:54.370 --> 00:07:58.720
So in 2016, the
Doodle team first

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:01.720
approached us with
this idea of, we

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:04.060
want to make a multiplayer game.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.180
So we had a couple of
meetings over the first year

00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:09.610
when they were still
kicking around the idea.

00:08:09.610 --> 00:08:13.750
And we identified
the same challenges

00:08:13.750 --> 00:08:16.960
that Jonathan already
mentioned, which is they

00:08:16.960 --> 00:08:20.350
had pretty limited experience
with multiplayer backends.

00:08:20.350 --> 00:08:22.030
But also, we
recognized there was

00:08:22.030 --> 00:08:25.720
a big strength, in that their
team was small and unified.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:31.210
The backend and the client
teams could very easily

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:34.900
go over to each
other's desk and help

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:40.150
make game design decisions that
could make the backend actually

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:44.059
simple enough to scale
for 100 million players.

00:08:44.059 --> 00:08:47.530
So in the end, they had
a very simplified scope,

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:49.720
which made it feel
like it was going

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:52.510
to be possible to build this,
even with a limited runway

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:53.980
and a small team.

00:08:53.980 --> 00:08:56.890
So the Doodle team was going
to be responsible for the game

00:08:56.890 --> 00:09:01.920
client and the game servers
for hosting the multiplayer

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.740
sessions, and Cloud--
myself, in this case--

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:09.640
took on a task to build
a prototype matchmaker,

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.540
and make sure that that would,
in general, work, and then

00:09:13.540 --> 00:09:16.330
eventually help them build a
production matchmaker on top

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:18.940
of Open Match.

00:09:18.940 --> 00:09:24.010
So the initial
design, we started

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:25.630
with very basic practices.

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:28.630
We wanted to start with what
worked for other companies.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:30.970
We knew that the
scale for a Doodle

00:09:30.970 --> 00:09:36.760
could be unbelievably huge,
even by the standards of--

00:09:36.760 --> 00:09:39.280
I've previously worked at
some pretty big game companies

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.340
on very successful AAA titles.

00:09:42.340 --> 00:09:44.860
Even compared to those, we
knew that the number of people

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:48.340
who show up to the Google
search landing page every day

00:09:48.340 --> 00:09:49.820
is well beyond that.

00:09:49.820 --> 00:09:53.230
So we had to keep
thoughts about how

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.820
to scale this kind of at
the front of our minds.

00:09:55.820 --> 00:10:00.790
So our first suggestion was to
get everything on Kubernetes

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:02.170
from the very beginning.

00:10:02.170 --> 00:10:04.630
We really liked,
as Jonathan said

00:10:04.630 --> 00:10:08.500
before, the fact that once
you're using Kubernetes

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:10.750
in your development
process, then you're already

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:12.777
in a really good place
where development looks

00:10:12.777 --> 00:10:14.860
a lot like what you're
going to put on production,

00:10:14.860 --> 00:10:18.700
and that can really help
accelerate your development.

00:10:18.700 --> 00:10:21.470
So putting everything in Docker
containers from the very get-go

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:23.980
was a big
recommendation of ours.

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.440
And then make the
only platform service

00:10:26.440 --> 00:10:29.890
for this game, which is the
matchmaker, as stateless

00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:34.470
as possible so that you can
scale up and down the API just

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.980
to handle however
many players show up.

00:10:37.980 --> 00:10:40.360
So to talk about
matchmaking, we really

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:42.210
wanted to help them
with the design,

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.440
so there were some
decision points.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.800
These were things
that we had to come up

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.050
with a good answer for
because it's really

00:10:50.050 --> 00:10:54.860
going to affect the complexity
of your matchmaking code.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:56.710
So how is the Doodle
going to handle

00:10:56.710 --> 00:10:58.630
if players don't show up?

00:10:58.630 --> 00:11:00.790
Does the dual support
backfilling games?

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.380
If a player leaves
mid-game, do you

00:11:02.380 --> 00:11:04.450
go find another
player to put in?

00:11:04.450 --> 00:11:06.340
Can players join in
progress if there's

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.650
free slots on the server?

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:10.270
Are there multiple
queues for people

00:11:10.270 --> 00:11:13.720
to queue in, to play with
different kinds of people?

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:17.410
Are there different regions that
you can queue for so that you

00:11:17.410 --> 00:11:20.480
only play with people locally?

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:26.680
So in pretty much every
decision in this slide,

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:29.740
the Doodle team went
for making something

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.900
as simple as possible,
which turned out

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:35.680
to really pay dividends because
the whole process went pretty

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:38.620
smoothly, as Brian is
going to describe later.

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:41.890
Doodle's considerations
being horizontal scalability

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:47.140
really did inform the
decisions to try and keep

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:50.290
more complex scenarios, like
allowing people to join games

00:11:50.290 --> 00:11:52.420
in progress, off the table.

00:11:52.420 --> 00:11:54.790
The one thing that
they did want in there

00:11:54.790 --> 00:11:57.648
was the ability to have
public versus private games.

00:11:57.648 --> 00:12:00.190
So you would be able to get an
invitation that you could send

00:12:00.190 --> 00:12:03.550
to your friends to have them
join directly your game,

00:12:03.550 --> 00:12:06.490
but we just ended up making
those into two completely

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:09.430
separate pools of players.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:11.980
Failover domains was a
thing that we were concerned

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:15.610
about-- like, what
happens if, heaven forbid,

00:12:15.610 --> 00:12:18.820
there's an internet
issue or something

00:12:18.820 --> 00:12:22.532
is unreachable for a certain
part of the player population?

00:12:22.532 --> 00:12:24.490
Well, it turns out they
were able to solve that

00:12:24.490 --> 00:12:27.340
just by putting everything
behind a Global Load Balancer,

00:12:27.340 --> 00:12:31.580
and Brian will speak to that
some more in another slide.

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:35.200
So quickly, let me go
through a breakdown of how

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:38.050
a single matchmaking instance
worked for the Doodle

00:12:38.050 --> 00:12:39.500
from left to right.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:43.240
So there's a client-facing
API that the game clients

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:44.770
talk to directly.

00:12:44.770 --> 00:12:46.510
That takes information
about that player

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:49.510
and writes it to
a Redis instance.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:54.070
Then the matchmaking logic comes
along and reads from Redis.

00:12:54.070 --> 00:12:56.950
Once it finds enough
players to make a match,

00:12:56.950 --> 00:13:00.310
it persists that match
to a Cloud Pub/Sub topic,

00:13:00.310 --> 00:13:03.820
which is actually
persistent data storage

00:13:03.820 --> 00:13:05.540
layer, at this point.

00:13:05.540 --> 00:13:08.650
So once you have put a
message into Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:13:08.650 --> 00:13:11.420
it is guaranteed
to be delivered.

00:13:11.420 --> 00:13:12.820
So there's no easy
way to lose it

00:13:12.820 --> 00:13:15.028
once you've already done
the work of matching players

00:13:15.028 --> 00:13:15.800
together.

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:18.640
So there's a server-facing
API that the game servers

00:13:18.640 --> 00:13:20.290
connect to.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:26.320
Once the game server connects,
it asks the server-facing API

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.120
to go and fetch a new match
from the Pub/Sub topic

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.940
and return it to
the game server.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:38.290
And at that point, the
players get a message

00:13:38.290 --> 00:13:40.450
out the client-facing
API, all the way

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:43.000
on the left, that
tells them the IP

00:13:43.000 --> 00:13:48.370
and how to connect over a
WebSocket to the game server

00:13:48.370 --> 00:13:50.000
directly.

00:13:50.000 --> 00:13:52.900
That is not pictured
on this slide,

00:13:52.900 --> 00:13:56.380
but there is a return
path for getting it back.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:14:00.060
For anyone here who has
actually looked at Open Match,

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.880
you may notice that there's some
differences in the way things

00:14:03.880 --> 00:14:05.710
are named here.

00:14:05.710 --> 00:14:08.980
So Doodle was using Open
Match before Open Match

00:14:08.980 --> 00:14:09.880
was even announced.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.660
So by the time we worked
closely with Unity

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.070
to get it out the door--

00:14:15.070 --> 00:14:17.920
the 0.1 version--
we tried to come up

00:14:17.920 --> 00:14:23.550
with slightly more generic
names for the API that

00:14:23.550 --> 00:14:25.620
did a better job of
describing scenarios

00:14:25.620 --> 00:14:28.200
that were not Doodle-specific.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.550
So now I'm going to
turn it over to Brian.

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.970
He's going to talk about
productionizing the Doodle.

00:14:40.230 --> 00:14:41.230
BRIAN MURRAY: All right.

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:42.730
Thanks, Joe.

00:14:42.730 --> 00:14:43.780
So I'm Brian Murray.

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:45.730
I'm a software engineer
on the Doodle team,

00:14:45.730 --> 00:14:48.130
and I was the lead backend
engineer for the Halloween

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:48.630
Doodle.

00:14:51.010 --> 00:14:54.527
So first, some unique challenges
we had to face along the way.

00:14:54.527 --> 00:14:56.110
With Doodles, we
don't have the luxury

00:14:56.110 --> 00:14:58.485
of a soft launch, where we
can gradually increase traffic

00:14:58.485 --> 00:14:59.740
over time.

00:14:59.740 --> 00:15:01.810
We basically have to
go from zero traffic

00:15:01.810 --> 00:15:04.990
to all of Google's search
instantaneously, which

00:15:04.990 --> 00:15:06.880
turns out to be difficult.

00:15:06.880 --> 00:15:09.700
And since we run
Doodles on Google.com,

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:11.650
any downtime would
be really bad.

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:14.050
It could put the
project in jeopardy

00:15:14.050 --> 00:15:16.870
because we can't exactly just
move Halloween back a couple

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:18.470
of days.

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:21.760
But luckily, we only had to keep
this thing alive for 48 hours.

00:15:21.760 --> 00:15:24.220
So unlike most
game developers who

00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:27.220
have to support a project
for months or years,

00:15:27.220 --> 00:15:28.990
we only had to stay
awake for two days.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.620
So I'll just briefly run through
our back end infrastructure.

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:37.840
You may remember a similar
diagram from earlier.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:40.810
This is what we started with.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:41.660
Then we met Joe.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:43.640
He built us a matchmaker.

00:15:43.640 --> 00:15:46.730
And this is essentially what
the inside of our clusters

00:15:46.730 --> 00:15:50.360
looked like at launch.

00:15:50.360 --> 00:15:52.130
When we needed to
scale up capacity,

00:15:52.130 --> 00:15:54.740
we picked regions around the
globe to start clusters in.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:57.890
We connected them to Cloud's
Global Load Balancer,

00:15:57.890 --> 00:16:00.500
and then we just let it balance
the traffic geographically.

00:16:00.500 --> 00:16:03.110
We didn't have any custom
logic for routing people

00:16:03.110 --> 00:16:06.075
to different regions.

00:16:06.075 --> 00:16:07.700
And when we needed
to scale it further,

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:09.110
we just added more clusters.

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:16.090
So here, we have a list of all
of the tools and technology

00:16:16.090 --> 00:16:18.520
that we used to
develop the backends.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:20.890
As you can see here,
everything that we used

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:23.080
is available externally.

00:16:23.080 --> 00:16:26.110
There's no Google-specific
internal magic

00:16:26.110 --> 00:16:28.900
behind the scenes, and even the
way we interacted with Cloud

00:16:28.900 --> 00:16:31.147
was very similar to how
an external customer would

00:16:31.147 --> 00:16:32.230
have interacted with them.

00:16:35.102 --> 00:16:37.310
So I'll go a little bit into
our development process,

00:16:37.310 --> 00:16:38.930
for those that are interested.

00:16:38.930 --> 00:16:42.170
We separated our development
into two GCP projects, one

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:44.480
for production, one
for development.

00:16:44.480 --> 00:16:46.490
And we kept all of
the resources separate

00:16:46.490 --> 00:16:48.050
between these
projects, which ensured

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:49.800
that anything we were
doing in development

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:52.490
wouldn't suddenly
break production.

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:54.610
And within our
development project,

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.770
we had our own clusters
for development.

00:16:57.770 --> 00:16:58.960
So I had a cluster.

00:16:58.960 --> 00:16:59.980
Jonathan had a cluster.

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:02.620
We had a cluster
for load testing.

00:17:02.620 --> 00:17:05.569
And it was really important that
we had no special configuration

00:17:05.569 --> 00:17:06.069
for these.

00:17:06.069 --> 00:17:08.770
They were basically
production clusters,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.829
but we wanted to make sure
that behavior in these clusters

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:15.089
was as close to
prod as possible.

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:16.839
And finally, we wanted
to make the process

00:17:16.839 --> 00:17:20.349
of creating and destroying
them really easy.

00:17:20.349 --> 00:17:22.180
Because when you end
up in a weird state

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:25.300
after hours of debugging,
the last thing you want to do

00:17:25.300 --> 00:17:28.680
is to have to figure out, how
do I fix this in real-time?

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:31.180
It's nice to be able to just
throw everything away and start

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:32.900
from scratch.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:37.500
So we wrote a couple of scripts
to make this process easy.

00:17:37.500 --> 00:17:38.300
OK.

00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:39.110
So we have a game.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:40.850
We have a plan for
how to scale things.

00:17:40.850 --> 00:17:44.310
How do we actually figure
out how many servers we need?

00:17:44.310 --> 00:17:46.040
So in our case, we
knew roughly how much

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:50.570
traffic to expect by looking at
numbers from previous Doodles.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:52.170
We knew how long our games were.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.890
And if we combined these two,
we could use some basic math

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:56.630
to estimate how
many people would

00:17:56.630 --> 00:17:59.100
be connected at a given time.

00:17:59.100 --> 00:17:59.900
So we did that.

00:17:59.900 --> 00:18:02.000
We extrapolated a little
bit, and we came up

00:18:02.000 --> 00:18:03.890
with an initial estimate.

00:18:03.890 --> 00:18:05.680
We knew our numbers
wouldn't be perfect,

00:18:05.680 --> 00:18:07.930
but we just hoped that they
would be close enough just

00:18:07.930 --> 00:18:10.380
to get a rough idea of how
much traffic to expect.

00:18:12.950 --> 00:18:13.450
OK.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:14.890
So now we have our
initial estimate.

00:18:14.890 --> 00:18:16.557
We need to figure out
if we can actually

00:18:16.557 --> 00:18:18.430
handle that much traffic.

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:20.560
So we wrote a custom client--

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:22.900
a headless client-- to
simulate the process

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:25.930
of connecting to the
matchmaker, joining into a game,

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:28.050
playing through it,
and then disconnecting.

00:18:28.050 --> 00:18:29.800
And then when we wanted
to run load tests,

00:18:29.800 --> 00:18:32.830
we just started a bunch of
these in a Kubernetes job,

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:35.650
pointed it at our
load test cluster,

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:38.200
and then let them run wild.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.240
And our strategy throughout
this whole process

00:18:40.240 --> 00:18:42.700
was to start really
small, only scale up

00:18:42.700 --> 00:18:44.830
when we were confident that
the system could handle

00:18:44.830 --> 00:18:47.000
a given amount of traffic.

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.640
So here, we have
a quick example.

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:51.773
We have 10 clients per second
pointed at a cluster here.

00:18:51.773 --> 00:18:54.190
When we're confident that it
can handle that much traffic,

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:57.400
we scale that up to 20.

00:18:57.400 --> 00:19:00.350
Looks good at 20,
so we got a 30.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:01.940
And then in this
example, we noticed

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:04.040
that, at 30 clients per
second, our matchmaker

00:19:04.040 --> 00:19:05.820
starts having issues.

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:08.660
So let's say our CPU
usage is spiking.

00:19:08.660 --> 00:19:09.410
We investigate.

00:19:09.410 --> 00:19:13.310
We decide that we just need more
matchmakers, so we add more.

00:19:13.310 --> 00:19:14.870
We rerun our tests.

00:19:14.870 --> 00:19:17.750
We make sure that it's stable
and, if everything looks good,

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:20.618
we carry on.

00:19:20.618 --> 00:19:22.660
And then, occasionally,
we would run longer tests

00:19:22.660 --> 00:19:25.202
because there are certain things
that you can only figure out

00:19:25.202 --> 00:19:27.220
after maybe 10 or
12 hours of load,

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:30.230
that you wouldn't be able
to see in an hour test.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:33.460
So we would run tests
for 12 hours or 24 hours

00:19:33.460 --> 00:19:38.940
to verify that these
issues weren't happening.

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:41.660
And finally, since our estimates
initially were pretty rough,

00:19:41.660 --> 00:19:43.850
we wanted to make sure
that we ran load tests well

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:45.650
above our peak traffic numbers--

00:19:45.650 --> 00:19:48.030
our expected peak
traffic numbers.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:50.720
And so in our case, we
scaled up our load tests

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.750
to double what we
expected at peak traffic,

00:19:53.750 --> 00:19:56.570
and that basically gave us
an idea of what would happen

00:19:56.570 --> 00:19:57.620
if the Doodle went viral.

00:20:00.620 --> 00:20:01.120
OK.

00:20:01.120 --> 00:20:03.340
So we're ready for production.

00:20:03.340 --> 00:20:05.917
At this point, we set up
monitoring and alerting

00:20:05.917 --> 00:20:07.750
so that we can have
some visibility into how

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:09.340
the launch is going.

00:20:09.340 --> 00:20:12.190
We switched from running Redis
internally in our clusters

00:20:12.190 --> 00:20:15.370
to using Cloud Memorystore,
and that provided us

00:20:15.370 --> 00:20:17.220
better guarantees
of reliability.

00:20:17.220 --> 00:20:20.087
It gave us people we could
talk to if issues came up.

00:20:20.087 --> 00:20:22.420
And then we also worked with
Cloud professional services

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:24.310
to come up with a launch plan.

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:26.590
They helped us with stuff
like capacity planning,

00:20:26.590 --> 00:20:29.530
reserving resources, setting
up on-call rotations,

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:32.270
that kind of stuff.

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:32.770
OK.

00:20:32.770 --> 00:20:33.530
Launch time.

00:20:33.530 --> 00:20:36.870
Time to turn everything
on and hope for the best.

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:38.990
So we wanted to spin up
our clusters far enough

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.090
ahead of time to ensure
that they were stable

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:43.465
before the Doodle
actually went live.

00:20:43.465 --> 00:20:44.840
On the other hand,
we didn't want

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.510
to give too much lead
time because running

00:20:47.510 --> 00:20:52.113
36,000 CPUs doing nothing
is kind of expensive.

00:20:52.113 --> 00:20:53.780
Since we were only
running for two days,

00:20:53.780 --> 00:20:56.813
we gave ourselves about three
hours to wrap everything up.

00:20:56.813 --> 00:20:58.730
I would have liked to
have maybe four or five,

00:20:58.730 --> 00:21:02.030
but it was almost enough.

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:05.570
From there, we just kept a
close eye on Stackdriver.

00:21:05.570 --> 00:21:09.320
And if any metrics came up
that we hadn't planned on,

00:21:09.320 --> 00:21:11.270
Stackdriver allowed us
to create dashboards

00:21:11.270 --> 00:21:14.090
for those in real-time,
which was really nice.

00:21:14.090 --> 00:21:16.070
We worked with Cloud
professional services

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:18.650
to fix any issues as they arose.

00:21:18.650 --> 00:21:21.380
And as I mentioned earlier,
we scaled our load tests up

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:24.430
to double our
expected peak traffic,

00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:26.810
while our average load ended
up being about double what

00:21:26.810 --> 00:21:27.720
we expected.

00:21:27.720 --> 00:21:31.010
So it was really valuable to
have seen how the system dealt

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:33.207
with this amount of
traffic beforehand,

00:21:33.207 --> 00:21:34.790
as opposed to having
to deal with that

00:21:34.790 --> 00:21:37.230
for the first time
on launch day.

00:21:37.230 --> 00:21:40.428
We had to add a couple more
clusters to compensate.

00:21:40.428 --> 00:21:42.720
Luckily, though, the process
for spinning those up only

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:44.500
took about 15 minutes.

00:21:44.500 --> 00:21:47.080
And so it ended up not
being that big of a deal,

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:50.340
and we were able to do
that without any downtime.

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.590
And other than that, the
launch went pretty smoothly.

00:21:55.440 --> 00:21:58.950
So just a quick summary
to wrap things up.

00:21:58.950 --> 00:22:01.410
Our backend and frontend
engineers worked very closely

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:04.110
together, and that
fact was very valuable

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:07.260
because the close communication
helped us avoid a lot of design

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:12.110
churn, and it kept our
final design simple.

00:22:12.110 --> 00:22:15.140
I know this is a Cloud-sponsored
talk, but as a team that

00:22:15.140 --> 00:22:17.090
doesn't normally think
about Cloud at all

00:22:17.090 --> 00:22:20.270
or work with Cloud at all, we
found it a really solid base

00:22:20.270 --> 00:22:21.890
for this type of project.

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:24.770
We never had to manage
any VMs, migrate services,

00:22:24.770 --> 00:22:26.390
deal with downtime.

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:29.720
And the cloud documentation made
Kubernetes surprisingly easy

00:22:29.720 --> 00:22:31.760
to learn.

00:22:31.760 --> 00:22:34.520
Speaking of
Kubernetes, Kubernetes

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:37.020
made our scaling
process a lot simpler,

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:40.410
which was really
useful on launch day.

00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:43.220
Once we had our
clusters configured,

00:22:43.220 --> 00:22:45.350
it was easy to just
start more of them

00:22:45.350 --> 00:22:47.600
to handle the
unexpected load, and we

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.640
could do that without downtime.

00:22:49.640 --> 00:22:52.250
And lastly, just a
shout out to Nita Meng,

00:22:52.250 --> 00:22:55.910
who is our contact from
Cloud professional services.

00:22:55.910 --> 00:22:57.380
She did a lot of
work to help make

00:22:57.380 --> 00:22:59.360
sure this launch went
smoothly, and she

00:22:59.360 --> 00:23:00.860
was a huge part of its success.

00:23:04.060 --> 00:23:06.370
And if you're interested
in learning more,

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:08.140
Google Cloud wrote
up a blog post

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.000
about the project, which
you can find if you

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.875
Google Halloween Doodle GCP.

00:23:11.875 --> 00:23:13.250
And if you want
to play the game,

00:23:13.250 --> 00:23:15.430
you can go to
Google.com/doodles,

00:23:15.430 --> 00:23:18.860
and it'll be in
our Archive page.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:20.060
Thank you.

00:23:20.060 --> 00:23:23.110
[APPLAUSE]

